      "title": "Adjunct Lecturer in Political Science & International Relations",
      "description": "Posted Date: 2025-11-01 Department: Politics and Intl Relations College: School of International and Public Affairs Job family: Adjunct Location: Modesto A. Maidique Campus About FIU Florida International University is a Top 50, preeminent public research university with 55,000 students from all 50 states and more than 140 countries, as well as an alumni network of more than 340,000. Located in the global city of Miami, the university offers more than 200 degree programs at the undergraduate, graduate and professional levels, including medicine and law. FIU faculty are leaders in their fields and include National Academy members, Fulbright Scholars and MacArthur Genius Fellows. A Carnegie R1 institution, FIU drives impactful research in environmental resilience, health and technology and innovation. Home to the Wall of Wind and Institute of Environment, FIU stands at the forefront of discovery and innovation. With a focus on student success, economic mobility and community engagement, FIU is redefining what it means to be a public research university. Job Information The Politics and International Relations department at Florida International University is seeking adjunct lecturers to prepare and deliver relevant instruction using a variety of teaching methods to undergraduate students obtaining a degree, certificate or minor in political science and/or international relations. Instruction may be face-to-face or online. The Department particularly needs instructors for classes in American Politics. Under the direction of the Department's Associate Chair or assigned mentor: Review course materials, catalog description, course objectives and learning outcomes, and syllabi prepared by full-time faculty. Read and review Section V of the Faculty Handbook on 'Teaching Policies and Resources' (http://academic.fiu.edu/docs/faculty_handbook.pdf). Develop course syllabus and outline to conform with university standards, department expectations and university class meeting schedule. Provide associate chair/mentor and department office manager with copy of course syllabus not later than three weeks before the beginning of the semester, if practicable. Select appropriate textbooks, notify department staff of book selections, and timely place book orders with university bookstore. Review any textbook or other course material changes with the associate chair/mentor and other full-time faculty teaching the course. Revise and update course content as needed. Facilitate class instruction, discussion sessions, presentations and other class activities. Teach the assigned course in accordance with learning objectives and course outline. Maintain records of enrollment, attendance (if appropriate), and grades. If face-to-face instruction, hold class each day for the full time allotted by the university. If on-line instruction, provide at least weekly Adobe Connect (or other university-approved technology) lectures with weekly assignments and timely feedback. Meet with the associate chair/mentor to discuss all aspects of the course including student progress and curriculum development. Evaluate student performance using a variety of measurement tools including but not limited to: examinations that include subjective and objective questions, research and analytical papers, group projects or oral presentations. Be available for student consultation through office hours or scheduled appointments (face-to-face or online), by phone or by email. Provide students with prompt feedback on assignments and course completion using Canvas or by returning graded materials to students. Timely submit final grades into FIU Panthersoft system and Canvas, if appropriate. Properly administer student evaluations of instruction (face to face) and encourage students to submit course evaluations (on-line). Submit all grade rosters for all students, any retained coursework, and other course materials to the department manager at the end of the semester. Please note that this pool is open for the 2025-2026 academic year and is reviewed based on departmental needs. Screening of applications will occur as positions become available and continue until positions are filled. Qualified candidates are encouraged to apply to Job Opening ID 536052 at careers.fiu.edu and attach their CV. This pool will remain open until October 31st, 2026. Minimum Qualifications Primary Instructor: Master's Degree in Political Science, International Relations or equivalent. Secondary Instructor, Course Assistant and Other Instructional: Bachelor's Degree in Political Science, International Relations or equivalent. Desired Qualifications Ph.D. in political science or international relations; or Ph.D. in field directly related to course content. Job Category Adjunct Pre-Employment Requirements Criminal Background Check How To Apply Prospective Employee If you have not created a registered account, you will be asked to create a username and password for use of the system. It is recommended that you provide an active/valid e-mail account as that will be the main source of communication regarding your status within the process. In this account, you are able to track your applicant status in \" My Applications \". To be considered eligible for the position as an internal candidate, departmental staff must meet minimum requirements of the position and be in good performance standing. Before you begin the process, we recommend that you are prepared to attach electronic copies of your resume, cover letter or any other documents within the application process. It is recommended that you combine your cover letter and resume/curriculum vitae into one attachment. Attached documents should be in Microsoft Word or PDF format. All applicants are required to complete the online application including work history and educational details (if applicable), even when attaching a resume. *This posting will close at 12:00 am of the close date. How To Apply Current 